  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe latest satellite imagery shows little in the way of cloud \ncoverage with high cirrus coming off a low pressure system over\nto our west which is starting to invade. These clouds will \nadvect in this evening and spread across the area overnight. The\nresult will be little to no change in the forecast other than \nfor some valley fog tonight mainly where areas decouple \nnoticeably across the southern coal fields and northeast deeper \nmountain valleys. Fog will not be deterred by the high clouds \nalthough stronger surface flow will deter much in the way of fog\nformation elsewhere. Have thrown in a little fog to the \nforecast near the Ohio River up by Parkersburg as well going \noff of forecast persistence.\n\nHigh pressure is settled in at the surface and keeping our\natmosphere capped along with plenty of dry air in the column. \nThis will prevent any precipitation through the rest of the \nwork week as this dominating surface high pressure system will \nkeep us shielded for now. Upper level ridging reinforces the \nfair weather out to this weekend. Plenty of southerly flow will \nkeep temperatures on a slow rise to above seasonable into the \nweekend. This will subsequently keep overnight lows warmer than \nwhat we have been observing to where the area does not have to \nworry about frost or freeze this week or the weekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA long stretch of dry weather comes to an end courtesy of a low\npressure system riding off a wave from Mexico. This southern \nstream system is forecast to impede the area on Sunday. Chances \nfor precipitation will spread across the entire area for a \nchance to get some impactful rain whether a wetting rain to\nmitigate potential fire concerns or an abundance of rain \npossibly raising some isolated flash flooding concerns slightly.\n\nAnomalously high PWATS for this time of year come into fruition\nby the impact timing of the aforementioned system. This system \nis forecast to take a northeasterly track up through the area \nand provided plenty of moisture to the area. This could in turn \ncreate the possibility of isolated flash flooding even though we\nhave been dry for so long. Mainly thinking about flood prone or\nvery low lying areas with poor drainage are of concern if \nvalues of precipitation increase across the area as we get \ncloser. \n\nOnly due to this system being southern stream along with\nbringing the bolstering deep south moisture to the area for \nSunday into Monday is this raising a small concern for hydro.\nAlso, this concern comes to play if the area is overrun by \nsufficient rainfall. As of now, nothing to worry about, but \nworth keeping on eye on and to see where the highest amounts of\nrainfall will be forecast to grasp a better understanding if\nflash flooding will be considered a real threat. Wouldn't be \nsurprised if WPC hashes out a marginal threat for excessive \nrainfall for the area from Sunday to Monday, however as of the \ntime of this writing this window reaches past the day 5 \noutlook.",
